(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-asthma-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Asthma Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 24-year-old woman has asthma symptoms 2 days a week, wakes at night with cough twice a month, uses albuterol 2 days a week, and has no limitation of activity. Her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 88% predicted with a normal ratio, and she had no exacerbations requiring oral steroids this year. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Intermittent","Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Severe persistent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms on 2 or fewer days a week, night awakenings 2 or fewer times a month, rescue use on 2 or fewer days a week, no interference, a 1-second volume above 80% with a normal ratio, and 0 to 1 steroid-requiring exacerbations a year all sit in the intermittent column. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman had asthma as a young child, then had no symptoms throughout adolescence. Over the past 2 months her episodic wheezing and night cough have returned. Which statement best explains her course?","choices":["Wheeze returning at 22 means cardiac asthma","Asthma can return in adulthood","Her first diagnosis must have been wrong","Asthma that remits in adolescence stays gone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asthma presents at any age, with a peak at 3 years; it may become asymptomatic during adolescence and may return in adulthood. Her childhood asthma, quiet teenage years and returning symptoms follow that pattern. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man has just finished a 5-day course of oral corticosteroids for an asthma exacerbation and feels well. He asks whether he must slowly taper the medicine. Which reply is correct?","choices":["He must taper over the next 2 weeks","He should continue it long term","He should switch to an inhaled form for 5 days","No taper is needed after a short course"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Systemic corticosteroids are used for control during exacerbations as a short course, and no taper is required. Long-term systemic therapy is different; it is reserved for severe or refractory cases at the minimum dose needed. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man reports asthma symptoms 4 days a week but not every day, wakes at night 3 times a month, uses albuterol 4 days a week but not daily, and notes minor limitation when hiking. His forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 84% predicted with a normal ratio. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Intermittent","Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Severe persistent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms and rescue use on more than 2 days a week but not daily, 3 to 4 night awakenings a month, minor limitation, and a 1-second volume above 80% with a normal ratio all fall in the mild persistent column. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman taking theophylline for asthma has nausea, vomiting, headache and a rapid irregular heartbeat. Her potassium is low and her glucose high. Which explanation best fits her presentation?","choices":["Theophylline toxicity","Albuterol side effects","Inhaled corticosteroid side effects","Aspirin-sensitive reaction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Theophylline has a narrow toxic-therapeutic range, and its toxic effects are nausea, vomiting, tachyarrhythmias, headache, seizure, hyperglycemia and hypokalemia, matching this woman's picture. That toxicity is why it is rarely used and needs close monitoring. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 71, 75, 81, 82, 92"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman with asthma notices that her wheezing and chest tightness flare at the same predictable point in every menstrual cycle and are mild the rest of the month. Which type of asthma describes this pattern?","choices":["Intrinsic asthma","Aspirin-sensitive asthma","Catamenial asthma","Occupational asthma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Catamenial asthma is asthma whose symptoms occur at predictable times during the menstrual cycle, as with this woman's monthly flares. It is one of the other types of asthma alongside occupational, cardiac, exercise-induced and aspirin-sensitive patterns. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with asthma and nasal polyps developed wheezing, facial flushing and a runny nose within an hour of taking aspirin last year. She now has painful knee osteoarthritis and asks for an anti-inflammatory pain reliever. Which drug is the most appropriate choice?","choices":["Naproxen","Diclofenac","Indomethacin","Celecoxib"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her wheeze, flushing and rhinorrhea after aspirin mark aspirin-sensitive asthma, so non-selective anti-inflammatory drugs must be avoided. Selective agents such as celecoxib or meloxicam may be used, which treats her knee pain without the cross-reaction. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 92, 93"},{"q":"A 15-year-old soccer player has cough, chest tightness and wheezing that start within 3 minutes after hard running, peak about 10 to 15 minutes later, and clear within an hour. She has no symptoms at rest or at night. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":["Moderate persistent asthma","Vocal fold dysfunction","Catamenial asthma","Exercise-induced bronchospasm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exercise-induced bronchospasm is common in children and adolescents, begins during or within 3 minutes after exercise, peaks in 10 to 15 minutes and resolves by 60 minutes, exactly this player's pattern, with no symptoms at rest or at night. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 94, 95"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman with allergic asthma triggered by cat dander is considering allergen immunotherapy against that single allergen. Which statement about this therapy applies to her?","choices":["It can be self-given safely at home","It works poorly against a single allergen","It is reserved for non-allergic asthma","It must be given in a controlled setting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Immunotherapy may be considered when there are allergic triggers and is most useful against a single allergen, as for her cat dander. It must be done in a controlled environment because it can induce bronchoconstriction and may require urgent treatment of complications. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has had stable, well-controlled asthma for 2 years. How often should his spirometry be repeated to monitor progression?","choices":["Every month","Every 3 months","Every year","Every 5 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Spirometry is best done before and after a short-acting bronchodilator and is repeated annually to monitor progression. For this man with stable, controlled disease, a yearly test tracks his lung function between routine follow-up visits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 16-year-old basketball player has cough and wheezing starting a few minutes into every practice, gone within an hour. Resting spirometry is normal. Which test best confirms the suspected cause?","choices":["Methacholine challenge","Allergy skin testing","Chest x-ray","Exercise challenge"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms starting soon after exercise and gone within the hour suggest exercise-induced bronchospasm, and normal resting spirometry is not diagnostic. Among the provocation tests, an exercise challenge is the one used when exercise-induced bronchospasm is suspected. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 58, 94"},{"q":"A 28-year-old pregnant woman with allergic asthma asks whether keeping her baby away from dust, pets and pollen will prevent the child from developing asthma. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Avoidance does not lower the risk","Strict avoidance prevents asthma","Early exposure guarantees asthma","Avoidance matters just for pet dander"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled allergens are common triggers and are implicated in sensitization, but avoiding allergens does not reduce the risk of developing asthma, and early exposure may even be protective by building tolerance. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man with asthma has symptoms 1 day a week, wakes at night once a month, uses albuterol 1 day a week, and has a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 91% predicted. In the past year, however, he needed 3 separate courses of oral corticosteroids for exacerbations. How should his asthma be classified?","choices":["As intermittent asthma","As persistent asthma","As well-controlled with no change","As exercise-induced bronchospasm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. His impairment is intermittent-level, but patients with 2 or more exacerbations requiring oral systemic corticosteroids in the past year may be considered the same as patients with persistent asthma, even without persistent-level impairment. Three steroid courses put him in the persistent risk row. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with moderate persistent asthma controlled on an inhaled corticosteroid now has hypertension and new stable angina. His cardiologist is choosing a medicine for both. Which drug class should be avoided in him?","choices":["Inhaled corticosteroids","Leukotriene modifiers","Beta blockers","Short-acting beta-2 agonists"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta blockers are listed with aspirin as medications that worsen asthma and should be avoided in asthmatic patients. For this man's angina and hypertension, another drug class is needed so his airways are not provoked. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man with long-standing asthma has had several exacerbations this year despite good inhaler technique and compliance. Testing shows a strong hypersensitivity to Aspergillus fumigatus with markedly raised immunoglobulin E and immunoglobulin G antibodies to the mold. Which diagnosis explains his worsening?","choices":["Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis","Steroid-resistant asthma","Occupational asthma","Non-compliance with the controller"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis is a hypersensitivity reaction to Aspergillus fumigatus that occurs almost exclusively in asthma or cystic fibrosis; exposure causes a vigorous immunoglobulin E and G response that produces acute asthma symptoms and exacerbations, as in this patient. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 63-year-old postmenopausal woman with refractory, poorly controlled asthma will need long-term oral corticosteroids to suppress her symptoms. Which addition reduces the bone loss from this therapy?","choices":["Montelukast","Cromolyn sodium","Calcium and vitamin D","Theophylline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When long-term systemic corticosteroids are needed, calcium and vitamin D supplementation should be considered to reduce bone mineral loss, since osteoporosis is a known side effect. Her postmenopausal status adds to the bone risk, and the minimum effective steroid dose is used. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl having spirometry for suspected asthma gives inconsistent, half-hearted blows, and her results vary widely between attempts. Which step is most appropriate before interpreting the test?","choices":["Report her best result as normal","Move straight to a methacholine challenge","Replace spirometry with a chest x-ray","Coach and encourage her, then repeat"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spirometry results depend on patient effort, so the patient may need coaching and encouragement to achieve good results. Her inconsistent blows make the current numbers unreliable, and repeating with coaching gives a valid test. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has asthma symptoms 3 days a week, wakes at night 3 times a month, and uses albuterol 3 days a week but not daily. She has minor limitation of activity. Her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 72% predicted with a ratio reduced by 4%. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Intermittent","Severe persistent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Her symptoms, night waking, rescue use and minor limitation fit mild persistent, but a 1-second volume between 60% and 80% predicted (72%) is a moderate persistent feature, and severity follows the most severe category in which any feature occurs. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man with asthma and a body mass index of 36 asks how his weight relates to his asthma. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Obesity matters just for exercise-induced symptoms","Obesity makes asthma harder to control","Obesity has no link to asthma","Obesity makes asthma milder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Asthma is more frequent in people with a body mass index above 30, and their symptoms are more difficult to control, although the mechanism is unknown. His body mass index of 36 places him in that group. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 15-year-old soccer player develops cough and wheezing within minutes of hard running, peaking about 10 minutes later and resolving within an hour. She is otherwise symptom-free. Which treatment should she use before exercise?","choices":["Inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist","Long-acting beta-2 agonist alone","Tiotropium","A short oral corticosteroid course"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her symptoms begin within minutes of exercise, peak at 10 to 15 minutes and resolve by an hour, the exercise-induced bronchospasm pattern. An inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist used before exercise decreases the bronchoconstriction. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 94, 96"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man with extrinsic asthma flares whenever he cleans his apartment, and allergy skin testing shows sensitivity to house dust mites. His controller and rescue inhalers are prescribed. Which non-drug measure should be added?","choices":["Limit his physical activity","Start allergen immunotherapy at home","Move from the city to a rural area","Reduce dust mite exposure at home"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-pharmacological asthma treatment is trigger avoidance and environmental control. Dust mites are the most common inhaled allergen trigger, and his skin test and cleaning-related flares identify it, so reducing his exposure at home is the added measure. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 38, 67"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man describes cough and chest tightness with cold air and smoke over several months. His spirometry is normal, with no meaningful change after albuterol. Asthma is still suspected. Which test is most appropriate next?","choices":["Chest x-ray","Repeat spirometry in 1 year","Methacholine challenge","Exercise challenge"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bronchial provocation testing with methacholine or histamine is useful when spirometry is not diagnostic, as here, where a typical history meets normal spirometry. His normal lung function also clears the rule that provocation is not done below 65% predicted. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman has spirometry for suspected asthma. Before a bronchodilator her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 61% predicted with a ratio of 55%; after albuterol it is 94% predicted, a 54% improvement. Which interpretation fits?","choices":["Poor patient effort","Reversible airflow obstruction","Fixed airflow obstruction","Normal spirometry"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A reduced 1-second volume and a reduced ratio before the bronchodilator show airflow limitation, and a 54% improvement after albuterol, far above the 12% threshold, shows reversibility. A positive bronchodilator response strongly supports asthma. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 57"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with moderate persistent asthma returns for a scheduled follow-up visit 2 months after starting an inhaled corticosteroid. He reports few symptoms. Which task belongs at this visit?","choices":["Repeat a bronchial provocation test","Stop his written action plan","Start a long-term oral corticosteroid","Review and update his action plan"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Follow-up visits assess and monitor control, review medications and compliance, adjust medications as needed, and review and update the action plan, then schedule the next follow-up. Good control does not remove these tasks. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 43, 90"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man with asthma is reviewing his written action plan with you. Which finding is a danger sign telling him to take his quick-relief medicine and go to the hospital or call an ambulance now?","choices":["Blue lips or fingernails","Coughing at night","Peak flow at 75% of personal best","Mild wheeze with the first signs of a cold"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The action plan's danger signs are trouble walking and talking because of shortness of breath, and lips or fingernails that are blue. Either one means take the quick-relief medicine and go to the hospital or call an ambulance now, without waiting. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 88, 89"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with severe persistent asthma has needed 4 courses of oral corticosteroids this year despite confirmed adherence and good technique with her inhaled corticosteroid and long-acting beta-2 agonist. Which step is most appropriate?","choices":["Refer to a lung or allergy specialist","Add theophylline","Start allergen immunotherapy at home","Stop the long-acting beta-2 agonist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With adherence and technique confirmed, her repeated steroid-requiring exacerbations make her asthma refractory. Monoclonal antibody agents are used by pulmonology and allergy specialists and reduced the need for corticosteroids in trials, so referral to a specialist fits. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 84, 91"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with moderate persistent asthma is given a peak flow meter to assess her control at home. When should she take her readings?","choices":["Each morning and each afternoon","Just when she is wheezing","At bedtime after exercise","Once a month at the clinic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Peak flow is measured in the morning before the bronchodilator and in the afternoon after it. A 20% change between those readings, or from day to day, indicates poor control, so this schedule lets her and her clinician see variation. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has asthma symptoms every day, uses her albuterol inhaler daily, wakes at night with symptoms twice a week, and has had to cut back some activities. Her forced expiratory volume in 1 second is 68% predicted with a reduced ratio. Which severity classification fits?","choices":["Intermittent","Mild persistent","Moderate persistent","Severe persistent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Daily symptoms, daily short-acting beta-2 agonist use, night awakenings more than once a week but not nightly, some limitation of activity, and a 1-second volume between 60% and 80% predicted with a reduced ratio all fall in the moderate persistent column. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 19-year-old college student has had episodes of wheezing, chest tightness and cough for 2 years, worse at night and after cleaning his dusty dorm room. He has allergic rhinitis and atopic dermatitis (eczema). Between episodes his lung exam is normal. Spirometry shows a reduced forced expiratory volume in 1 second that improves 18% after albuterol. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":["Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease","Vocal fold dysfunction","Extrinsic asthma","Intrinsic asthma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Episodic wheeze, chest tightness and cough worse at night, a dust trigger, allergic rhinitis and eczema (the atopic triad), a normal exam between episodes, and an 18% rise in the 1-second volume after albuterol (12% or more is a positive response) make allergic, extrinsic asthma the diagnosis. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slides 25, 35, 53"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman using an inhaled corticosteroid for moderate persistent asthma has hoarseness and white plaques on her tongue. She uses her inhaler without a spacer and does not rinse afterward. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Use the inhaler just during attacks","Use a spacer and rinse the mouth after each dose","Stop the inhaled corticosteroid","Switch to a long-term oral corticosteroid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hoarseness and oral thrush (oropharyngeal candidiasis) are local side effects of inhaled corticosteroids, and they may be decreased by using an inhalation chamber and rinsing the mouth with water after dosing. She keeps her preferred first-line controller. — PAJ5500 CMSI Asthma MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 75"}]};
